Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from the book "Nick and Me"
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. SEATTLE PIZZERIA OFFICE - DAY

A modest office overlooking a busy Seattle street. Rain streaks the tall windows. PETER, mid-thirties, fit with short brown hair and a calm gaze, sits at a desk stacked with order sheets and a laptop. He wears a simple button-down and looks directly at camera.

PETER
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book we read in school. No. My real Big Brother. Nick.

(SOUND: distant rain on glass, low hum of kitchen ovens)

PETER
He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls. Face like a puzzle held together with glue-gun gunk. Scars on his chin and left eyebrow. When Nick walked into a bar or the gym, people moved.

He stands, walks to the window. City traffic moves below.

PETER (cont'd)
We lived with Ma after Dad left. Fifth-floor walk-up on Kirk Street in Milwaukee. Nick could haul six grocery bags up those stairs like it was nothing. I carried the rest.

(SOUND: faint echo of footsteps on stairs, grocery bags rustling)

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - DAY

The same fifth-floor apartment, worn furniture, old TV playing a game show. MA, late fifties, frail with brown hair and tired eyes, sits in an overstuffed chair. Young PETER, early twenties, skinny with messy brown hair, enters carrying pizza boxes. NICK, mid-twenties, broad-shouldered with reddish-brown hair, scars, and a commanding presence, follows, arms loaded with bags.

NICK
What’s Ma doin’ back in the bedroom at this time of day?

PETER
I don’t know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn’t be sleeping in the middle of the day like that. That’s how old people go downhill.

(SOUND: TV game show applause, distant city traffic)

NICK (cont'd)
They start sleepin’ all day, and pretty soon they don’t want to go outside or eat or nothing.

PETER nods, ashamed. MA looks up glassy-eyed, confused.

MA
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

PETER
Ma, we don’t go to church anymore. Your pills are right here.

(SOUND: pill bottles rattling on counter)

Nick shoots Peter a hard look. Peter looks away.

CUT TO:

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

Busy Milwaukee sidewalk. PETER walks toward the parlor door in his delivery uniform. SIONNA, mid-twenties, pale skin, short brown hair, light blue eyes, backpack, fish necklace, trips on the cracked cement and brushes into him.

PETER
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She brushes off her black V-neck shirt. They stand awkwardly.

PETER
I work here. What’s your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

PETER
Sionna? That’s different. What does that mean?

SIONNA
My mom and dad named me after an Irish goddess. Something about the River Shannon.

Her cool blue eyes hold his. Peter is transfixed.

PETER
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want to go for a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

She writes her number on a slip and hands it over. She walks away, glances back over her shoulder.

(SOUND: city traffic, her footsteps fading smoothly like ocean waves)

PETER watches, mesmerized.

PETER (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. She was intense.

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Dimly lit living room. MA rummages through pill bottles lined on the counter, mumbling. PETER enters, worried.

PETER
Ma, what’s the matter?

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today?

PETER
Ma, your pills are right in front of you. We don’t go to church anymore.

MA stares glassy-eyed, then snaps back.

MA
Oh, right. I see them. I don’t know what got into me.

(SOUND: pill bottles clicking, low TV murmur)

Peter looks concerned. He grabs his coat.

PETER
I’m going down to Joe’s for a beer.

CUT TO:

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Crowded bar, Packers game on TV. PETER sits at the counter. NICK enters, slaps him on the back.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What’s shakin’?

PETER
Nothin’.

NICK
I can tell you’re hidin’ something.

PETER tells him about Ma. Nick gets angry.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Why don’t you take her to the store or something? Holy Christ, do something with her before we have to pay for a nursing home.

(SOUND: football crowd roar on TV, clinking beer bottles)

NICK (cont'd)
I don’t have the money for that shit.

Peter looks down, guilty. Nick gives him a look that could punch through walls. Peter pays and leaves.

CUT TO:

INT. LIBRARY - NIGHT

Quiet, wood-floored library with tall windows and hanging lights. PETER approaches the desk.

PETER
How do I take a book home from here?

LIBRARIAN
Do you have a library card?

She helps him get a temporary one. Peter wanders to the classics section, pulls "Wuthering Heights."

(SOUND: pages turning, soft hum of fluorescent lights)

PETER (V.O.)
I picked out “Wuthering Heights” by Emily Bronte. It was about Heathcliff and Catherine. So far it was a little like a soap opera. These two really loved each other.

He checks it out and walks home in the cold.

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Peter in bed, reading. He falls asleep. Dream sequence: lush forest, PETER in medieval tunic on horseback with SIONNA (in period dress, long brown hair). They ride to a sunny field, embrace, kiss. Her eyes are the lightest blue.

PETER (V.O.)
Hold!

He bolts upright, sweating.

PETER
What the hell was that?

(SOUND: heavy breathing, distant city wind rattling windows)

CUT TO:

INT. JAVA HUT COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Busy college coffee shop. PETER and SIONNA at a small table. She has a backpack. Her light blue eyes draw him in.

SIONNA
What are you reading right now?

PETER
Wuthering Heights. It’s the story of this guy and girl and how they grew apart but were really in love with each other.

SIONNA
Yea, I know. I read it.

She talks passionately about symbols, ghosts, the moors, wild love that destroys.

SIONNA (cont'd)
A love that never changes will destroy you.

Peter listens, awakened.

(SOUND: coffee grinder whirring, quiet chatter of students)

PETER (V.O.)
Something inside of me was coming awake. She made me feel smarter just by being around her.

They make a dinner date for Friday.

CUT TO:

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

Sunlit studio with purple mats. About twenty people, including PETER in t-shirt and shorts, and SIONNA. They move through poses. Peter struggles, shirt falling, but finishes in Savasana.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

PETER
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
No. You caught on very quickly. Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

(SOUND: soft breathing, gentle yoga music fading to calm silence)

Peter looks relaxed, transformed.

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Cluttered living room. NICK stomps around, red-faced. MA looks upset in her chair. PETER enters.

PETER
What’s going on Nick?

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

PETER
I was with someone. I met a girl.

NICK
A girl. That’s worth all this? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

They argue. Nick slaps Peter across the head.

NICK (cont'd)
Don’t you EVER talk that way about MY mother.

Nick storms out, slamming the door.

(SOUND: loud door slam, echoing footsteps retreating, Ma's shallow breathing)

PETER rubs his head, furious but calm.

CUT TO:

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Sterile hospital room. MA in bed, monitors beeping. PETER sits by the window. SIONNA visits, they walk outside briefly. Peter falls asleep in the chair.

Dream: chaotic battlefield. PETER as soldier stands over a dying man who whispers "Save her." NICK runs up and stabs him in the chest.

PETER (V.O.)
That you are, little brother.

Peter wakes gasping. Ma dozes.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeping faster then steadying, distant hospital PA announcements)

PETER
Nick had killed me.

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Living room. MA in chair watching TV. PETER sets out pill container. NICK enters, beet-red, wild hair.

NICK
You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

They argue about Sionna. Voices rise. Nick slaps Peter again.

NICK (cont'd)
Don’t you EVER talk that way about MY mother you little fuck.

Nick storms out.

(SOUND: raised voices overlapping, sharp slap, door slam reverberating)

PETER
Who needs Nick? Me and Ma and Sionna would be just fine without him.

CUT TO:

EXT. BAR - NIGHT

Crowded bar, loud rock music, pool tables. NICK, PETER, BOB, SAMMY inside. Lindsey with new boyfriend. Nick confronts them. Peter steps in.

PETER
Nick, you don’t have any problem with this guy. Leave it.

NICK draws back to hit Peter. Joe and bouncer intervene. Chaos erupts. Peter runs out into the night.

(SOUND: AC/DC blaring "Have a Drink on Me", shouting, breaking glass, footsteps pounding pavement)

PETER runs to Sionna's.

CUT TO:

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Cozy living room with green suede sofa. PETER bursts in, tells SIONNA everything. They lie on the couch. He rests his head in her lap.

PETER
The first time I met you, I felt like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too, honey. Me too.

They fall asleep. Dream: mystical lake. SIONNA rises from water like an angel, protects them from giant fiery NICK with invisible force. Nick shrinks and vanishes.

(SOUND: wind howling then calming, water lapping, ethereal music swell then silence)

They wake disoriented at 3 a.m.

CUT TO:

INT. MILWAUKEE APARTMENT - MORNING

Quiet apartment. PETER finds blinking answering machine. Messages from police and hospital. He calls, learns of the accident. Ma dead. Nick injured.

PETER
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

He breaks down sobbing.

(SOUND: answering machine voice droning, Peter's raw sobs, distant siren)

CUT TO:

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Bright ICU. NICK in bed, bruised, tubes everywhere. PETER stands in doorway, refuses to enter.

PETER
Die you fucking jackass. Die.

(SOUND: monitors beeping steadily, ventilator hiss)

CUT TO:

INT. SIONNA'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Hallway. NICK confronts SIONNA, grabs her neck, pulls switchblade.

NICK
If it wasn’t for you messing around with my brother, everything would be cool. You come up with some money and get the fuck away from my brother.

Peter enters, struggles. Nick stabs Sionna. Blood flows. Peter applies pressure, calls 911. Nick flees.

(SOUND: struggle grunts, blade flick, wet stab, sirens approaching)

PETER
Sionna!

CUT TO:

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Sterile booth with thick plexiglass. Older, graying NICK sits opposite. PETER on this side, calm and strong.

PETER
I am no longer interested in seeing you. You killed my mother. You held me down my entire life. You tried to kill the love of my life. But I forgive you, Nick. I forgive you because it sets me free.

NICK tears up.

NICK
I’m so sorry. You’re a big man. I’m a small one.

PETER
After all is said and done, I still love you for being my brother.

(SOUND: muffled prison echoes, soft click of phone receiver, distant buzz of doors)

They hang up. Peter walks out free.

CUT TO:

INT. SEATTLE PIZZERIA OFFICE - DAY

Back in the office. PETER stands by the window, rain stopped. Sun breaks through.

PETER
That was Nick and me. I own a pizza chain now. President Olson. I learned to let go. To forgive. To live.

He smiles at camera.

(SOUND: rain stopping, birds outside, uplifting music swell)

PETER (cont'd)
God bless us, every one.

FADE OUT.

THE END
